@page "/docs/getting-started"
@layout DocsLayout
@using MudBlazor

<PageTitle>Getting Started — AgentBlazor Docs</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-article">
    <section class="docs-hero">
        <p class="docs-hero__eyebrow">Getting Started</p>
        <h1 class="docs-hero__title">From package install to a route-scoped workflow.</h1>
        <p class="docs-hero__lede">
            The shortest successful path is still one package, one workflow, one route, and one chat surface.
            If you want the fastest baseline install, use the CLI first. If you want full control, wire the runtime by hand.
        </p>

        <div class="docs-actions">
            <MudButton Variant="Variant.Filled" Color="Color.Primary" Href="/docs/cli">Use the CLI path</MudButton>
            <MudButton Variant="Variant.Outlined" Color="Color.Secondary" Href="/demo/workflows/response-orchestration?reset=true">See the live workflow demo</MudButton>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>1. Optional CLI-first path</h2>
                <p>Use this when you want preview-first install guidance and scaffolded baseline wiring.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>dotnet tool install --global AgentBlazor.Cli --version 0.1.0-preview.9
agentblazor init ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp
agentblazor scaffold ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp --provider openai
agentblazor scaffold ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp --provider openai --approve
agentblazor doctor ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp
agentblazor validate ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Important:</strong> scaffold can write provider registration and baseline files, but you still supply the actual API keys
            and environment-specific configuration values yourself.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>2. Install the package</h2>
                <p>This is the manual runtime path and remains the cleanest way to understand the surface area.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>dotnet add package AgentBlazor</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>3. Configure services</h2>
                <p>The most validated production path today is OpenAI via <code>options.UseOpenAI(...)</code>; Azure OpenAI uses the same runtime path.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>using AgentBlazor;
using MudBlazor.Services;

builder.Services.AddMudServices();

builder.Services.AddAgentBlazor(options =&gt;
{
    options.UseOpenAI(
        apiKey: builder.Configuration["OpenAI:ApiKey"]!,
        model: builder.Configuration["OpenAI:Model"] ?? "gpt-5.4-mini");

    options.ConfigureBuilder(agentBuilder =&gt;
    {
        agentBuilder.AddWorkflow&lt;OperationsCapabilities&gt;(
            "Operations Agent",
            agent =&gt;
            {
                agent.WithDescription("Handles workflow assessment and remediation.");
                agent.WithRoutePrefixes("/ops");
            });
    });
});</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Azure OpenAI:</strong> use <code>UseAzureOpenAI(endpoint, deploymentName, apiKey)</code>, or pass a
            <code>TokenCredential</code> such as <code>DefaultAzureCredential</code> for managed identity.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>4. Add the host shell</h2>
                <p>The exact files vary by host shape. Standard Blazor Web App and hosted WebAssembly are the main automatic paths today.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-callout-grid">
            <div class="docs-callout">
                <h3>Standard Blazor Web App</h3>
                <pre class="docs-code"><code>// Components/App.razor
&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="@@Assets["_content/MudBlazor/MudBlazor.min.css"]" /&gt;
&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="@@Assets[AgentBlazorAssetPaths.Css]" /&gt;
...
&lt;script src="@@Assets["_content/MudBlazor/MudBlazor.min.js"]"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="@@Assets[AgentBlazorAssetPaths.Js]"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

// MainLayout.razor
&lt;MudThemeProvider /&gt;
&lt;MudPopoverProvider /&gt;
&lt;MudDialogProvider /&gt;
&lt;MudSnackbarProvider /&gt;</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-callout">
                <h3>Hosted WebAssembly server + client</h3>
                <p>The CLI can infer the companion client project from server <code>ProjectReference</code> entries and patch the standard server
                startup path plus safe client files such as <code>_Imports.razor</code>, <code>wwwroot/index.html</code>, layout, and page files.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-callout">
                <h3>Advanced hosts</h3>
                <p>Legacy <code>_Host.cshtml</code> server apps and other recognizable custom hosts stay review-first. The CLI will add safe files,
                then tell you which startup, shell, or layout locations still need manual wiring.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>5. Map endpoints</h2>
                <p>The runtime endpoint must be mapped after the app is built or the chat UI will render without a working backend.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>var app = builder.Build();

app.MapRazorComponents&lt;App&gt;()
    .AddInteractiveServerRenderMode();
app.MapAgentBlazorEndpoints();</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Hosted WebAssembly note:</strong> the same server-side endpoint mapping still matters. The client UI files live in the companion
            client project, but the runtime endpoint is still exposed from the server host.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>6. Define one capability class</h2>
                <p>Use attributes for semantic actions and let the runtime handle approvals, structured results, and next-step suggestions.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>using AgentBlazor.App;
using AgentBlazor.Attributes;

[AgentCapability("operations",
    Name = "Operations Workflow",
    Description = "Assess workflow readiness and prepare remediation.",
    Category = "Workflow")]
public sealed class OperationsCapabilities(IOperationsService ops)
{
    [AgentAction("Assess current workflow readiness")]
    public Task&lt;CapabilityResult&gt; AssessReadinessAsync()
    {
        var summary = ops.GetReadinessSummary();
        return Task.FromResult(CapabilityResult.Success(summary) with
        {
            NextActions = ["Explain blockers", "Prepare remediation"]
        });
    }

    [AgentAction("Prepare remediation draft", RequiresApproval = true)]
    public Task&lt;CapabilityResult&gt; PrepareRemediationAsync(
        [AgentParam("Target area")] string area)
    {
        var draft = ops.PrepareRemediation(area);
        return Task.FromResult(CapabilityResult.Success(draft));
    }
}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>7. Render a chat surface</h2>
                <p>Use the floating widget for ambient help or the full surface when chat should be part of the page layout.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>@@using AgentBlazor.Components

&lt;AgentChatWidget
    Title="Operations"
    Placeholder="Ask the agent to assess, prepare, or advance the workflow..."
    Width="30rem"
    Height="68vh" /&gt;</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Use <code>AgentChatSurface</code> instead</strong> when you want the chat to stay embedded inside a workflow page or dashboard layout.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>8. Verify the install</h2>
                <p>Use the runtime and CLI checks together.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>dotnet run
agentblazor doctor ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp
agentblazor validate ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-inline-list">
            <span>Assess the workflow</span>
            <span>Explain blockers</span>
            <span>Prepare the draft</span>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-note">
            See the <MudLink Href="/demo/components">component explorer</MudLink> for the current deterministic UI wrappers and return to the
            <MudLink Href="/docs">docs overview</MudLink> for the install map.
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
